interestingly, `DataDictionary<T>` is probably the _only_ dictionary implementation in LEAN that actually uses a vanilla dictionary under the covers instead of a `ConcurrentDictionary<TKey, TValue>` -- just some info -- when using `ConcurrentDictionary<TKey, TValue>` you _never_ want to call the `Values` or `Keys` property as it forces the dictionary to take a global lock. @StefanoRaggi went through the whole codebase a ways back and updated things  in ways that might not be intuitive, but are great for performance.

it's good style to place guard clauses at the very top of the scope. In this case, there's no value in parsing `sentiment` and `exchange` just to get to a bad `#text` value to skip the entire `ticker` -- but again -- I think most of the code in this loop collapses -- I recommend _briefly_ reviewing those docs, reading this code it looks like you're going against the grain of the library.

I'm gonna share something else here totally unrelated only because it's one of the coolest features of the library `JToken` side -- I actually went and found an [SO question](https://stackoverflow.com/a/29782523/1582922) I could answer using the feature so I would never lose it!! Basically you can provide a string expression, such as "symbols[*].ticker" which would work on an object like:
``` js
{
  symbols: [
   { ticker: 'ticker-1' },
   { ticker: 'ticker-2' },
   { ticker: 'ticker-3' },
   { not-ticker: 'ticker-1' }
  ]
}
```

and it would happily ignore the `not-ticker` -- pretty nice feature when working using the JToken library. _That_ being said, in general, the JToken side of the house is typically used when you don't have the contracts coded up (custom class w/ `[JsonProperty]` attributes.
